<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monastery of San Juan de la Peña</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">San Juan de la Peña is a monastery in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaca">Jaca</a>, in Aragon, built beneath a great overhanging rock. Its origins lie in the 10th century or earlier, and in 1071 it became the first monastery in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> to adopt the Roman liturgy. Its royal pantheon holds the tombs of early kings of Aragon, and its Romanesque cloister is famous for its carved capitals.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monastery-of-san-juan-de-la-pena.jpg" alt="Monastery of San Juan de la Peña" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elemaki · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SanJuandelaPe%C3%B1a.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
